  "rule": {
    "title": "Modals in the past",
    "explanation": "Para deducir qué pasó usamos modal + «have» + participio:\n- «must have» = seguro que pasó: «You must have left it somewhere.»\n- «can't have» / «couldn't have» = seguro que no pasó: «I can't have left it in the taxi.»\n- «might», «may» o «could have» = quizá pasó: «You might have left it in the taxi.»\n\nOtros usos muy frecuentes:\n- «could have» = era posible, pero no pasó: «I could have lost the whole demo.»\n- «could have» = reproche: «You could have told me!»\n- «should have» / «shouldn't have» = crítica o arrepentimiento: «You should have kept it in your bag.»\n\n«Needn't have» + participio: hiciste algo que al final no hacía falta. «Didn't need to» + verbo: no era necesario, y normalmente no se hizo:\n- «You needn't have worried. The laptop was safe.»\n- «We didn't need to call the police.»\n\n«Should have» + participio también es lo que esperábamos y no ha pasado:\n- «The parcel should have arrived by now.»\n\nOjo: siempre «have» + participio, nunca «had» ni el pasado simple («must have gone», no «must have went»).",
    "table": {
      "headers": [
        "Modal + have",
        "Significado",
        "Ejemplo"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "must have",
          "seguro que pasó",
          "He must have forgotten."
        ],
        [
          "can't have",
          "seguro que no pasó",
          "She can't have seen it."
        ],
        [
          "might / could have",
          "quizá pasó",
          "They might have left."
        ],
        [
          "could have",
          "pudo pasar y no pasó",
          "I could have fallen."
        ],
        [
          "should have",
          "crítica, arrepentimiento",
          "You should have asked."
        ],
        [
          "needn't have",
          "no hacía falta (y se hizo)",
          "You needn't have come."
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
